{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app/checkout_read.go at exact head a5c0889fa3e08f048eb1b09057af26da62ad3882 on request/lane-worktree-identity (pushed), one commit over main 359b211c, clean merge-tree onto current main c0108be8 (tree 8226bc07). New file, and the substance of this head's answer to the second changes-requested verdict 91c50bf9 on 643ab9c5, ratified 2d590023. CheckoutRead is one captured observation of this repository, shared by every judgment a worktrees request reaches: the checkout listing, the bounded ref inventory and whether that read finished inside its bound, the configured remote name, one three-second deadline and one 65,536-step budget. CaptureCheckouts takes it for a request; captureAround takes the same capture around a listing a caller already holds, so the single-judgment entry points get one ref read, one deadline and one budget by the same mechanism rather than a second one. graph() builds a traversal over the captured inventory and charges it to the captured budget. Close releases the deadline; Exhausted reports whether the budget or the deadline is already spent.\n\nWhy it exists, stated at the type: reading the listing or the ref inventory a second time answers about a different repository whenever a branch or a checkout moves in between, and two answers that disagree about which commit a branch is at are not two views of one world. Two budgets have the same defect one level up -- each bounds its own judgment and neither says what the request costs, so a request could spend twice the bound its documentation names and still report that every limit held.\n\nWhat is shared is the observation and the bound, never the conclusion. The type says so and the code holds it: the cleanup classification reads no grade and no association table, and the association reads no classification. An unsigned source trailer must not become cleanup authority, and a cleanup verdict must not decide what a commit claims.\n\nAdviseCheckouts is added at e962fa7b under the third changes-requested verdict 5b80e91e on 557ef676, ratified ffd7ffc6, which found that exhaustion after classification still published the candidate list. It is the whole of what a worktrees request may publish from one captured read: it runs ClassifyCheckouts, then AssociateCheckouts, then asks the read whether it finished. If the shared budget failed or the shared deadline or request context is done, everything both judgments produced is withheld -- unknownWorktrees empties the candidate list, sets every classification to unknown with the reason and clears Row, Rows, Approved, LandedInto and RemoteContains, and unknownAssociationTable sets every grade unknown, clears Governing and marks the table incomplete with the same reason, which is now the one constant exhaustedRead. The adopted design requires the whole batch's advice to be discarded on exhaustion, and the whole batch is the response rather than the judgment that happened to notice: publishing an answer because it was computed before the budget ran out offers a deletion candidate derived from a read that never finished looking for the thing that would have protected it. The step can only turn an answer into unknown, never the reverse, and it reads no claim. It was also claimed to be the only way any caller could obtain both answers. That was false and is corrected at this head under the fourth changes-requested verdict e389d856 on e962fa7b, ratified 8e5d50ee: ClassifyCheckouts and AssociateCheckouts remain exported and the tests call them separately. What is true, and what the doc comment now says, is narrower and checkable -- AdviseCheckouts is the one response-disposition step, and handleWorktrees obtains its pair only through it.\n\nThe disposition predicate is corrected at this head under the same verdict, which reproduced the defect on the previous head with a positive control: it asked only read.Exhausted(), so an association stopped by a bound of its own left the cleanup advice published beside it, and a receipt-backed settled checkout was still offered as a candidate while the table said complete=false with the tip-limit reason. The question asked of the pair is now whether either judgment failed to finish under the conditions it was given: the shared budget or deadline spent, or the table incomplete for any of its own reasons -- the tip limit, the per-tip depth limit, a captured tip this repository does not hold, an unreadable or over-bound ref inventory, or cancellation inside the association. All are the same kind of fact, a read that did not finish. The reason carried into the withheld response is the table's own where it has one and the exhaustion reason otherwise, so unknownWorktrees now takes that reason rather than hard-coding exhaustion, and a reader is told which bound stopped the read. New path in the merge target. The artifact at e962fa7b is the outside-target predecessor this head replaces; it is retired separately with this artifact as its successor.","body":{"branch":"request/lane-worktree-identity","commit":"a5c0889fa3e08f048eb1b09057af26da62ad3882","path":"internal/app/checkout_read.go","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9c9d6d97fd2a034a5684deddfcec7dd094885728, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a4c569b1cddfb0db72f9239488851f19a291906f stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03270b167c8f14b55a31919b2489715ee513b20f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca and 127 more"}}